<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aryabhatiya</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/499">499 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Aryabhatiya is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> astronomical and mathematical treatise composed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aryabhata">Aryabhata</a> in 499 CE. In 121 verses it covers arithmetic, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algebra">algebra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trigonometry">trigonometry</a> and astronomy, gives an approximation of pi of 3.1416 and proposes that the Earth rotates on its axis. It was a foundational text of Indian mathematical astronomy and influenced later Indian and Islamic scientists.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aryabhatiya.jpg" alt="Aryabhatiya" width="320" height="106" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Krishnachandranvn · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Description_of_Kuttaka_in_Aryabhatiya.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The Aryabhatiya is the only known surviving work of the Indian mathematician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aryabhata">Aryabhata</a>. Its 121 verses fall into four sections. The Gitikapada (13 verses) sets out the large units of time, kalpa, manvantara and yuga, and gives a table of sines in a single verse. The Ganitapada (33 verses) covers mensuration, arithmetic and geometric progressions, shadows cast by the gnomon and simple, quadratic, simultaneous and indeterminate equations. The Kalakriyapada (25 verses) deals with units of time, the positions of the planets on a given day, the intercalary month and a seven-day week.</p><p>The Golapada (50 verses) treats the celestial sphere, the ecliptic and equator, the shape of the Earth, the cause of day and night and the computation of eclipses. The verses are terse and mnemonic, and their study was probably meant to be guided by a tutor. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aryabhata">Aryabhata</a> used letters of the alphabet to form number-words, consonants giving digits and vowels place value.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The work uses a geocentric model in which the motion of each planet is governed by two epicycles, a smaller manda (slow) and a larger śīghra (fast) one. B. L. van der Waerden suggested that a heliocentric model lay beneath it, a view Noel Swerdlow and others rejected as contradicting the text. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aryabhata">Aryabhata</a> held that the Moon and planets shine by reflected sunlight and explained the causes of solar and lunar eclipses. His sidereal year of 365 days 6 hours 12 minutes 30 seconds is 3 minutes 20 seconds longer than the modern value. Historians judge his formulas for the volumes of a pyramid and a sphere to be wrong, though Elfering argued for a different translation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Twelve commentators on the work are known besides anonymous ones, from Prabhakara (c. 525) and Bhaskara I (c. 629) to Parameshvara and Nila-kantha in the 15th century and Kodanda-rama, writing in Telugu, around 1854. An anonymous Arabic translation, the Zij al-Arjabhar, appeared around 800 and was studied around 820 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-khwarizmi">Al-Khwarizmi</a>, whose book on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> numerals later helped bring the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-arabic-numeral-system">Hindu-Arabic numerals</a> into use in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Aryabhata's methods have remained in continuous use for fixing the Panchangam, the Hindu calendar.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aryabhatiya” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Aryabhata, whose <b>Aryabhatiya</b> dates to 499 CE, approximated pi to four decimal places and proposed that the earth rotates on its axis.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
